  "query_preview": "..Evening synoptic analysis shows a large ridge of high \npressure more or less centered from the Desert Southwest into the\nFour Corners region. Given the strength of the ridge, 700mb \ntemperatures remain anomalously warm. In fact, the evening weather\nballoon sampled a 700mb temperature of approximately 18.35C, well\nabove the 90th percentile for this time of year, and more or less\nat the daily max. In turn, it has been another hot day across the\nregion with widespread 100F readings at lower elevations. Aside \nfrom the heat, modest amounts of moisture pivoting around the \nridge have yielded some isolated to scattered convection across \nportions of southern and southwestern Utah this afternoon and \nevening. With near stationary motion, these have resulted in \nperiods of heavy rain, but also with the lower levels still a bit \ndry, some gusty outflow winds as well. With lack of daytime \nheating though, expect things to remain suppressed through what \nappears to be a mild overnight period.\n\nThis weekend the overall weather pattern does not appear to shift\nappreciably. First, excepting precipitation and associated cloud \ncover, forecast highs only look to decrease at most a degree or so\nday to day. With overnight temperatures also remaining very mild,\nespecially with any lingering cloud cover, the cumulative effects\nof heat will be amplified, and pose a risk to those without \naccess to cooling or proper hydration. Widespread Extreme Heat \nWarnings and Heat Advisories remain in effect through the weekend \naccordingly. In terms of precipitation chances, the primary \nchanges appear to be a slight north and eastward expansion \nSaturday and especially Sunday as moisture continues to work into \nthe region. Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nstill anticipated to form in the afternoon and evening hours, \nposing a threat of periodic heavy rain, lightning, and gusty \nerratic outflow winds. In terms of flash flood potential, the WPC \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook highlights much of southwest Utah in a \nMarginal Risk (level 1/4) Saturday, with the Marginal Risk area \nthen expanding to include more or less all of the forecast area. \nAs has been the case, especially with limited storm motion, the \nthreat will be highest over rain sensitive basins such as recent \nburn scars, slot canyons, typically dry washes, and slickrock \nareas.\n\nMuch of the upcoming week looks to see a monsoonal pattern \nmaintained as the ridge of high pressure if anything only becomes \nbetter centered over the Four Corners region, allowing a favorable\nmoisture tap into Utah and southwest Wyoming. PWAT anomalies \nareawide sit roughly around 125-150% of climatological normal, \nwith ensemble mean values peaking around 150-175% of normal \nsomewhere in the Wednesday/Thursday time frame. The WPC Excessive \nRainfall Outlook continues to maintain a widespread Marginal Risk \narea highlighting much of the region Monday and Tuesday (as far as\nthe outlooks go in time at this point), and suspect the area will\nsee a continued excessive rain/flash flood threat more or less \nthroughout the upcoming week. As is typical, this threat will be \nmost pronounced at those rain sensitive basins, though periods of \nheavy rainfall elsewhere may still pose localized risks at time. \nIn regards to timing, generally expect the diurnal cycle to \nlargely dominate with afternoon/evening convective development, \nthough more subtle vorticity maxima rotating around the periphery \nof the ridge may offer areas/periods of high coverage; a detail \nthat is not easily resolved this far out. Aside from the flood \nthreat, there does appear to be faster jet flow at areas \nespecially further north near the ID border, which may offer a bit\nmore shear and support for storm organization. Would anticipate \nthese places to see at least some potential for a few storms with \nstrong winds and hail as well. With the increased monsoonal \npattern, temperatures will continue to trend downward, offering \nsome relief to the heat.",
